

The discussion narrows the work into **four main investigative themes**, with the first three appearing to be the immediate priorities. The team is not being asked simply to prove that BitMEX behaved badly; they want evidence that can answer **duty → failure → alternative action → causation/loss**. 

### 1. Did BitMEX fail to maintain an “orderly market”? — **Highest priority**

This appears to be the central issue. The 2020 Terms of Service are described as saying that HDR had responsibility for maintaining an orderly market and discretion to halt trading because of market disruption or relevant external factors. The team wants to determine what **“orderly market” actually meant in 2020**, what conditions would reasonably justify intervention, and whether the March 12–13 conditions crossed that threshold. 

The analysis should concentrate on three questions:

**A. Should BitMEX have halted/suspended trading?**
Was XBTUSD so dislocated, illiquid, volatile, or liquidation-driven that a reasonable exchange would have temporarily stopped trading?

**B. Should BitMEX have changed its Mark Price methodology?**
This is particularly relevant to your work on Mark Price. They specifically raise whether BitMEX should have changed the basis of the Mark Price to prevent the unusually large differential that developed.

**C. What else could BitMEX reasonably have done?**
Don't limit the analysis to "halt trading" versus "do nothing." Identify other controls that could have maintained an orderly market.

This is why your work on **XBTUSD, Mark Price, liquidation price, bankruptcy price, and the liquidation engine** is foundational—it establishes what BitMEX's system was actually doing when the market became stressed.

